{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 4,
  "estimated_minutes": 25,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must distinguish Axia NetMedia as guarantor from its bankrupt network-operator subsidiary and understand why MTC seeks continued performance pending resolution of the underlying breach dispute."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The listed defects concern ordinary incorporation-by-reference and contract-heading principles, not the FCC authorization or technical network-operation issues discussed elsewhere in the opinion."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding the incorporation dispute requires reading Guaranty §4.6 against Network Operator Agreement §§11.1–11.2 and checking the Guaranty's Article II structure."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Opinion lines 32–35 expressly explain why §4.6's heading and differing dispute procedures do not prevent incorporation of §11.2, although the ruling arises on a preliminary-injunction motion."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "d01 and d03 describe opposite sides of the same incorporation holding, while d02 isolates the heading-and-placement argument supporting Axia's resistance to that holding."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d03: The reviewer must compare the two agreements' dispute procedures to understand why the court treats §4.6's express differences as carve-outs rather than obstacles to residual incorporation. It also requires distinguishing that incorporation-language issue from d01's classification of continued performance as a dispute-resolution provision.",
  "reason": "Assign this case to a general contract reviewer comfortable comparing related agreements; telecommunications expertise is unnecessary for the listed defects. The main task is tracing how the Guaranty's residual incorporation language brings in the operator agreement's continued-performance obligation despite different dispute procedures. The court states its construction plainly, so targeted reading should suffice without reviewing the network's technical provisions or the FCC history in depth. The reviewer should distinguish the overlapping incorporation defects and the separate heading argument, and should not mistake the injunction ruling for a final resolution of the underlying breach claims."
}
